/**
@defgroup api_reference Public API
@brief Everything reachable from `#include <batchlas.hh>`, plus the two opt-in SYCL headers.

Every entry point enqueues on a batchlas::Queue and returns a batchlas::Event;
nothing is readable until the queue (or the event) is waited on. The backend is
taken from the Queue, options are designated-initialiser structs, and scratch
comes from the Queue's workspace arena. The conventions shared by all entry
points are in @ref md_docs_2cpp-api.

@{

@defgroup api_core Queues, devices and events
@brief batchlas::Queue, batchlas::Device, batchlas::Event and SYCL interop.

@defgroup api_matrix Matrices, views and vectors
@brief Column-major batched containers and the non-owning views every entry point takes.

@defgroup api_enums Enumerations and scalar concepts
@brief Transpose, Uplo, Side, Diag, Backend and the scalar concepts that constrain every template.

@defgroup api_options Option structs
@brief Designated-initialiser option structs accepted by the entry points.

@defgroup api_blas2 BLAS level 2
@brief Batched matrix-vector operations.

@defgroup api_blas3 BLAS level 3
@brief Batched matrix-matrix operations: gemm, trsm, trmm, symm/hemm, syrk/herk, syr2k/her2k.

@defgroup api_factorizations Factorizations and linear solvers
@brief Cholesky and LU factorizations, their solves and inverses.

@defgroup api_qr QR and orthogonal transforms
@brief Householder QR, forming and applying Q, and batched orthogonalisation.

@defgroup api_eigen Symmetric and Hermitian eigensolvers
@brief syev and its tiers, selected-range syevx, and the iterative eigensolvers.

@defgroup api_tridiag Tridiagonal and band reduction
@brief Reduction to tridiagonal form and the tridiagonal eigensolvers (steqr, stedc, stebz, stein).

@defgroup api_svd Singular value decomposition
@brief gesvd and the bidiagonal pipeline stages.

@defgroup api_sparse Sparse (CSR) operations
@brief Sparse-dense products, ILU(k) and CSR test-matrix generators.

@defgroup api_extra Norms, condition numbers and utilities
@brief Matrix norms, condition estimates, inversion and scaling helpers.

@defgroup api_linalg Convenience layer (batchlas::linalg)
@brief Value-returning, NumPy-like wrappers over the entry points.

@defgroup api_dispatch Backend dispatch and vendor seams
@brief How a call reaches a backend: the Queue's backend, owning-argument forwarding, and the
`backend::*_vendor` declarations the vendor families call.

Which kernel runs for a shape is decided by flat kernel selection (developer docs:
@ref api_selection; design: @ref design_flat_selection; per-op families and tables:
@ref selection_tables). Measurements behind each op's choices: @ref perf_evidence.

@defgroup api_workspace Workspace and memory
@brief The per-Queue workspace arena, leases and BumpAllocator sizing.

@defgroup api_errors Errors and convergence status
@brief The exception hierarchy and per-item info reporting.

@defgroup api_config Configuration, environment and tuning
@brief Runtime settings captured from the environment and the tuning constants.

@defgroup api_device_blas Device-side group BLAS
@brief In-kernel work-group and sub-group BLAS templates (`#include <batchlas/blas/device.hh>`, needs `-fsycl`).

@defgroup api_internal_helpers Kernel helpers (installed, not API)
@brief Headers installed because public headers include them. Not a stable interface.

@}
*/

/**
@defgroup api_selection Kernel selection (developer)
@brief How an op picks the kernel that runs: kernel families, per-device tuned tables,
`select::choose`, pins, the trace, the coverage instrument and the vendor seam.

This is library-internal code under `src/select/` and `src/ops/<op>/`; none of it is installed.
The design, its rules R1-R8 and the record of how it was built are on
@ref design_flat_selection. Every op's families and shipped tables are listed on the generated
@ref selection_tables page, which is the authoritative inventory; this group documents the
mechanism they share.

@section selection_one_decision One decision per op, in one file

A public entry point decides what runs in `src/ops/<op>/<op>.cc` and nowhere else. It validates
its arguments, builds a table key from the call, and hands the op's candidates and its `can_run`
predicate to `select::run()`, which picks one choice, opens the trace and coverage scope, and calls
the op's `launch` visitor with it. The op's `<op>_buffer_size` asks `select::pick()` the same
question with the same key, so the workspace query and the call can never size for different
kernels (rule R5).

@section selection_families Families, choices and spellings

A *family* is one kernel driver, written as a small struct: a static `name`, a static `fields`
array naming its integer knobs, `values()`, `from()` and `operator==`. A family with no knobs
derives from `select::NoFields`. An op's *choice* type is a `std::variant` of its families, so a
choice is a value, and it has one spelling everywhere (`family[:field=value]...`, e.g.
`lpanel:panel=8`): in tables, pins, trace lines, coverage rows and test names
(`select::to_string()`, `select::parse()`). Anything a driver derives from the shape and the
device stays derived inside the driver and is never a field.

The op's `choice.hh` lists every compiled choice once in `candidates<T>()`. That list is what the
tuner times, what a pin must name and what a table row may contain, and its order is the tie-break
order: simpler kernels first and native kernels before `vendor`, so near-ties go to the native
kernel. A field-less choice lists its families in variant order (`select::all_of()`).

@section selection_opspec What an op declares once: OpSpec

`select::OpSpec` holds the op's `Op` enumerator (its name in tables, pins and coverage), the vendor
library group its `vendor` family calls (`select::Lib`), and its `select::Rules`: the last-resort
order, by generality, used when no table row names a runnable choice. The default is `blocked`,
then `vendor`, then the rest in candidate order.

@section selection_can_run can_run is correctness only

`can_run(choice, device)` is false exactly when that kernel would throw or give a wrong answer for
the call (rule R3); it never means "slower". Speed lives only in the tables (R4). A speed
threshold in `can_run` would make a pinned kernel unreachable and leave a shape with nothing to run
in a vendor-free build. Each clause should be the argument check at the top of the driver it
guards, and every candidate is pinned in a test on shapes that straddle its limits (R7).

@section selection_table_format Tables, lookup and borrowing

One plain-text file per (op, dtype, device), `tuned/<op>.<dtype>.<device>.txt`, embedded at build
time and parsed once; a directory named by `BATCHLAS_TUNED_DIR` replaces a built-in table of the
same name. The header names the keys (`# keys: uplo:exact n:log:3 batch:log`); each row is one
measured shape followed by every candidate timed there, fastest first. Inventory and provenance:
@ref tuned_tables_readme.

- **Nearest row** (`select::Table::nearest()`): the rows matching the longest prefix of the
  `:exact` keys, then the smallest weighted \f$ \sum_i w_i\,|\log_2(\mathrm{row}_i/\mathrm{key}_i)| \f$
  over the `:log` keys.
- **Borrow order** (`select::tables_in_borrow_order()`): the device's own table; then tables of the
  same family, nearest architecture at or below first; then other families. A device with no table
  borrows and warns once per op (R8). A CPU device never borrows a GPU table.
- **The walk**: in each table in borrow order, the first entry of the nearest row that is a
  candidate and passes `can_run` wins. If none does anywhere, the last-resort order decides; if
  nothing can run at all, `choose()` throws.

@section selection_pins Pins

`BATCHLAS_<OP>_ROUTE` is consulted on every call, from the settings snapshot (a raw `::setenv` is
not seen until `detail::reload_settings()`; `ScopedEnvVar` triggers one). The value is trimmed and
lower-cased, then means `auto`, `native` (the best runnable non-vendor choice), `vendor`, or a
spelling. A spelling that does not parse, is not a compiled candidate, or
fails `can_run` throws `std::invalid_argument` (R6); `native` and `vendor` fall back to the
automatic choice with a one-time warning when nothing in their class can run. Tests pin with the
RAII `select::ScopedPin`, which wins over the environment on its thread.

@section selection_trace_coverage Trace and coverage

`BATCHLAS_SELECT_TRACE=1` prints one stderr line per decision, indented under its parent op, with
a tag naming the table used (`[sm_120]`, a borrowed table, `override`, `fallthrough`,
`last resort`, `pinned`). `BATCHLAS_COVERAGE_OUT=<path>` makes `batchlas::coverage` write one CSV
per process: `linked` rows (what this build contains), `reached` rows (what calls actually ran,
with the choice spelling) and `miss` rows (calls nothing could serve). A `linked` row is never
evidence that a kernel runs. Instrument details and defects: the "Dispatch: the coverage
instrument" section of @ref md_docs_2perf_2dispatch.

@section selection_vendor_seam The vendor seam

`select::has_library<B>(lib)` is a compile-time answer to "is this op's vendor library in this
build for backend B", and it sets `select::Device::has_vendor`, which is all a `vendor` family's
`can_run` reads. The op's `vendor` arm is an `if constexpr` on it, so with the library absent the
vendor call is not compiled at all and `select::no_vendor()` records a coverage `miss` and throws
`NoRouteError` instead. That is what keeps `-DBATCHLAS_ENABLE_VENDOR_BLAS=OFF` an ordinary build;
see @ref md_docs_2design_2vendor-independence.

@section selection_add_op Adding an op

Write `src/ops/<op>/choice.hh` (families, `candidates<T>()`, `OpSpec`, key names, tuner grid) and
`src/ops/<op>/<op>.cc` (key, `can_run`, `launch`, workspace, the public entry point and its
`*_buffer_size`, the instantiations); add tables for every dtype on every shipped device; give the
tuner a spec in `tools/tune/<op>_spec.cc`; pin every candidate in a test. The step-by-step version
is in @ref md_docs_2extending. An op directory without a `choice.hh` still selects by hand; the
generated @ref selection_tables page lists exactly the ops that have one.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_selection_ops Per-op choices
@ingroup api_selection
@brief The vocabulary header of every op under `src/ops/<op>/`: its families, `candidates<T>()`,
`OpSpec`, table keys and tuner grid.

Each `src/ops/<op>/choice.hh` adds itself to this group. Which families rank first on which device,
and where each table came from (measured, converted or transcribed), is on the generated
@ref selection_tables page, and the measurements behind each op's families are on its
`docs/perf/` evidence page.
*/
